<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  This app stores personal financial records. Nothing about the database should
  leave the device unless the user explicitly exports it from the Backup screen.

  android:allowBackup="false" is not sufficient on its own. Google documents that
  on Android 12 (API 31) and above, on devices from some manufacturers, it stops
  cloud backup but can still leave device-to-device transfer enabled for the app:

    https://developer.android.com/identity/data/autobackup
    "On devices from some device manufacturers, specifying android:allowBackup=
     false disables cloud-based backup and restore (such as Google Drive backups)
     but doesn't disable device-to-device transfers for the app."

  Every domain is therefore excluded from cloud backup and device-to-device
  transfer explicitly, and every domain is listed rather than relying on the root
  domain's recursion, so the intent does not depend on how the backup engine
  walks its domains. A missing section is not a safe default for these two: omit
  one and that mode is fully enabled, which is why neither is omitted.

  Android 16 QPR2 (API level 36.1) added a third mode, cross-platform transfer,
  configured with a <cross-platform-transfer> child element. Whether that mode is
  on by default is read two ways and Google's own material supports both, so no
  single reading carries the decision below.

    The guide says that if there are no rules for a backup mode, that mode is
    "fully enabled for all content". Read literally, that would cover this
    section too. But the AOSP eligibility check admits only apps "that have
    explicitly opted-in", and the developer blog says an app "can opt-in to
    participate", which makes adding the element the thing that joins the path.

  Either way there is nothing for it to carry. This mode moves data between the
  same app on two platforms, and the guide makes <platform-specific-params> with
  bundleId and teamId a required part of the section, there "to guarantee that
  data is only transferred to the specified app on the other platform". This app
  is Android-only, no iOS counterpart exists, and none is declared, so no
  transfer can be matched and none happens. Adding the element would instead
  assert a counterpart that does not exist.

  Do not add one. If an iOS version is ever built, add the section then with its
  real bundleId and teamId, and update the test that pins this file.

  Devices running Android 11 or lower need nothing further here: device-to-device
  transfer is an Android 12 feature, and allowBackup="false" fully disables cloud
  backup on those versions.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
